Z||000000||Philphine||09-28-2001||11:06 AM||||how warm dose it need to be to lay fiberglass? i have to do some out side.<P> i started working on some speaker pods just as the weather cooled some here. my first layer took so long to set i wondered if i hadn't put enought hardener in. i wanted to do a little before i went to work each day but now i'm waiting for the weekend 'cause i need to leave the door open until it sets (i'm working in the dead pedal area). since i have every thing taped off and covered (molding the back of the pod to the corner) i need to finish before i can start driving the car again.<P>i know tempeture makes a difference in the cure time from laying stuff in the summer and doing it in the winter in a kind of cool basement. this was at about 65-70 degrees. how much colder can it get and still allow me to work on this? thanks for any help.   ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||09-28-2001||11:16 AM|||| An infrared heat lamp will do wonders as it not only warms things up but the IR will also "kick" the fiberglass. I had some pieces that took a long time to set and still stayed sort of gummy. I put them in the sun, not so much for the heat but rather the IR and then they set right up in an hour or so.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Joe Torma||09-28-2001||01:04 PM||||Use lots of hardner!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||09-28-2001||02:42 PM||||My mistake, it's the ultra violet (UV) from the sun that kicks the glass.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Sage||09-28-2001||04:19 PM||||70-72 is ideal with no humidity.  Phil is right on with the lights.  You can use a heat lamp to speed things up.  Lay it up like you would usually, then when you are done put the heat lamp on it, but not too close,(it could start smoking!)  They tell you in the instructions for the resin that you may have to adjust the amount of hardner to the outside temp.  When it's cooler, you have to add more, sometimes up to twice what you would use under ideal conditions.  You can only go so far with this theory though, you CAN get too much in.  At some point it will do the opposite intended, that is it will NEVER harden!  You run the risk of having it harden while you're trying to lay it up too!  I have actually seen a resin container burst into flames because the temp changed 10 degrees between the time the resin was mixed and the time they were finished using it!  General rule of thumb; if it's 10-15 degrees below optimum temp, increase your hardner content by about a third, if it's colder than that, don't lay it up, or move to where you can control the temp!  When I want something to kick fast, I lay it up normally, then move it into the sun.  It generally kicks right now, or at least within a very short period of time.<BR>Another little footnote:  When using glass and resin, you really have to "get with the program", that is if you mixed your stuff right, you don't have unlimited time to play with it!  About 18 minuets is ideal, then it should be starting to kick.<BR>Hope this helps a little.<P>Have fun||||reg||1
Z||000005||ray b||09-28-2001||05:25 PM||||ANY HEAT will help even a normal lite bulb<BR>that will raze the temps 5-10 deg. on a boat repare in the water i used oil lamps[no ac]<BR>an other trick is a super hot[high mek dose] on a thin layer put on the work if it is not kicking after temps drop but tooo much mek will fry the work [make the part brittle]so use a very thin layer and only if the work will not kick with a little heat or you can't heat it.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||3
